# Data Structures: Organizing Data for Speed

> Finding one item in a million can take a million steps — or one.
> Data structures are the art of choosing that arrangement.

## 1. Why Structure Choice Matters

Finding one name among a million unsorted records by scanning takes
up to **1,000,000 comparisons**. Sorted, **binary search** needs
about **20** (since 2^20 > 1,000,000). In a **hash table**, roughly
**1**. Same data, same machine — different structure, five orders of
magnitude less work.

[definition]
A **data structure** is a way of organizing data in memory so the
operations you care about — search, insert, delete, traverse — are
cheap, while accepting that others become expensive.
[/definition]

## 2. Arrays vs Linked Lists

| Aspect | Array | Linked list |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Memory layout | One contiguous block | Nodes scattered, each with a pointer |
| Access i-th item | O(1) — index arithmetic | O(n) — walk from the head |
| Insert/delete at front | O(n) — shift every element | O(1) — rewire pointers |
| Insert at end | O(1) amortized (dynamic arrays) | O(1) with a tail pointer |
| Search (unsorted) | O(n) | O(n) |
| Cache friendliness | Excellent — sequential memory | Poor — pointer chasing |
| Memory overhead | None | One pointer per node |

```text
Array:  [ 12 ][ 5 ][ 8 ][ 3 ]    one block, index math
List:   (12)->(5)->(8)->(3)     nodes + pointers
```

[tip]
CPU caches stream contiguous memory, so arrays beat linked lists in
real programs far more often than the big-O table suggests.
[/tip]

## 3. Stacks, Queues, and Deques

| Structure | Rule | Core ops (all O(1)) | Classic uses |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Stack** | LIFO — last in, first out | `push`, `pop`, `peek` | Call stack, undo, browser back |
| **Queue** | FIFO — first in, first out | `enqueue`, `dequeue` | OS ready queue, print jobs, BFS |
| **Deque** | Both ends open | push/pop at both ends | Sliding windows, work stealing |

```python
stack = []
stack.append(1)   # push
stack.append(2)
stack.pop()       # pop -> 2 (the last one in)
```

## 4. Hash Tables

A **hash table** stores key–value pairs. A **hash function** converts
a key into an array index, so a lookup is: hash the key, jump
straight to that bucket.

| Aspect | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| Average lookup/insert/delete | O(1) |
| Worst case | O(n) — every key collides |
| Collision | Two different keys hash to the same bucket |
| **Chaining** | Each bucket holds a small list of colliding items |
| **Open addressing** | Probe nearby slots for the next free spot |
| Load factor | Items ÷ buckets; too high → resize and rehash |

[important]
Hash tables buy O(1) speed by *forgetting order*: they answer "is
this key here, and what is its value?" but cannot cheaply list keys
in sorted order or answer range questions — that is a tree's job.
Caches, de-duplication, and compiler symbol tables all lean on them.
[/important]

## 5. Binary Search Trees

A **binary search tree (BST)** orders every node: left subtree keys
are smaller, right subtree keys larger.

@diagram tree id="bst-example" layout="tree" aria="Binary search tree holding keys 8, 3, 10, 1, 6 and 14"
  "8" -> "3"
  "8" -> "10"
  "3" -> "1"
  "3" -> "6"
  "10" -> "14"
@/diagram

Searching follows one path from the root, and each step discards
half the remaining keys when the tree is balanced. An **in-order
traversal** (left, node, right) visits keys in **sorted order**.

[warning]
A BST stays fast only while it stays *balanced*: insert 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
in order and the tree degenerates into a linked list — O(n) search.
The fix is automatic rebalancing.
[/warning]

## 6. Balanced Trees and Heaps

| Tree | Idea | Kept fast by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **AVL tree** | Strict balance — sibling heights differ by at most 1 | Rotations on every insert/delete |
| **Red-black tree** | Looser color rules, fewer rotations | Used inside `std::map`, Java's `TreeMap` |
| **Heap** | Parent always ≤ children (min-heap) — not a search tree | Priority queues |

A **heap** answers one question in a hurry — *"what is the smallest
item?"* — not "where is this key?", making it the engine of priority
queues, Dijkstra's shortest path, and heapsort.

| Operation | BST (balanced) | Heap |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Search by key | O(log n) | O(n) |
| Insert | O(log n) | O(log n) |
| Find minimum | O(log n) | **O(1)** |
| Delete minimum | O(log n) | O(log n) |

## 7. Choosing the Right Structure

| Your need | Best structure |
| --- | --- |
| Fast lookup by key, order irrelevant | Hash table |
| Sorted order, range queries | Balanced BST (AVL / red-black) |
| Undo, backtracking, most-recent-first | Stack |
| Fairness — first come, first served | Queue |
| Repeatedly extract the smallest/largest | Heap |
| Fixed shape, index math, cache-friendly scan | Array |

## Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Array vs list | Index O(1) vs O(n); cache loves arrays |
| Stack / queue | LIFO undo vs FIFO fairness |
| Hash table | O(1) average by key; chaining or probing |
| BST | In-order traversal is sorted; O(n) if unbalanced |
| Heap | O(1) peek at the minimum; priority queues live here |
